<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Pre-travel care for the deployment (Z71.84)</content>. Vaccines complete for departure: the yellow fever certificate is valid, and hepatitis B dose 2 and influenza vaccine are given today. Malaria is transmitted in parts of French Guiana, where P. vivax is most common, followed by P. falciparum, with chloroquine resistance; CDC lists atovaquone-proguanil, doxycycline, mefloquine and tafenoquine. Atovaquone-proguanil is chosen because it is taken only from one to two days before to seven days after the malaria areas. After months in a P. vivax area, primaquine terminal prophylaxis is advised, and his G6PD is normal. Dengue and chikungunya are spread by day-biting mosquitoes and typhus by fleas, lice and mites, so vector avoidance is the main protection. Pre-departure blood count normal: hemoglobin 14.1 g/dL, platelets 236 x10^3/uL. [S]<list><item>Atovaquone-proguanil one tablet daily with food, from one to two days before entering a malaria area until seven days after leaving.</item><item>Primaquine 30 mg base daily for 14 days, starting when he leaves the malaria areas.</item><item>EPA-registered repellent on skin, permethrin-treated clothing and bed nets, screened or air-conditioned quarters; long sleeves at dawn and dusk; flea and lice control aboard and clean bedding.</item><item>Food and water: safe water, well-cooked food, washing hands; azithromycin 500 mg daily for up to three days for severe diarrhea or diarrhea with fever or blood, with oral rehydration.</item><item>Any fever during travel or in the months after return means a malaria test the same day; tell the doctor about the travel.</item><item>Carry the medical summary, the yellow fever certificate, and all medicines in their original packs.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Relapsing P. vivax malaria, after radical cure (B51.9)</content>. No relapse seven months after radical cure. [S]<list><item>Continue the fever action plan; the terminal primaquine course also covers any new P. vivax exposure.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Relapse surveillance and the fever action plan</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No fever in seven months; plan extended to travel and after return</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Pre-departure blood count</td><td>Once, before a long deployment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Hemoglobin 14.1 g/dL, platelets 236 x10^3/uL</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Malaria chemoprophylaxis choice, dosing and interactions</td><td>Before travel</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Atovaquone-proguanil prescribed; no interacting medicines; take with food</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Terminal prophylaxis (presumptive antirelapse therapy) for prolonged P. vivax exposure</td><td>After leaving the malaria areas</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Primaquine 30 mg base daily for 14 days prescribed, G6PD normal</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Yellow fever certificate valid on arrival</td><td>Before travel</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Vaccinated eleven weeks ago; certificate valid</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Hepatitis B vaccine series</td><td>Two doses one month apart</td><td>Completed today</td><td>HepB-CpG dose 2 today</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Hepatitis A vaccine dose 2</td><td>Six to twelve months after dose 1</td><td>Due</td><td>Due after the deployment; dose 1 gives protection for the trip</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Influenza vaccine</td><td>Each season</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Given today</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Vector avoidance for dengue, chikungunya and typhus</td><td>Before and during travel</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Repellent, permethrin-treated clothing, nets, screens; flea and lice control aboard; no dengue vaccine is recommended for travelers</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Chikungunya vaccine</td><td>Before travel, if an outbreak notice applies</td><td>Due</td><td>CDC lists a chikungunya outbreak in French Guiana and recommends vaccination for travelers to outbreak areas; to be decided at a short visit a month before departure against the notice then current</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Food and water precautions and travelers&apos; diarrhea self-treatment</td><td>Before travel</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Advice given; standby azithromycin prescribed</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>No further malaria relapse in the six months after radical cure. Outcome by 2026-10-13: No fever in the seven months since finishing primaquine [S]</item><item>All required and recommended travel vaccines given, with a valid yellow fever certificate, at least ten days before departure. Outcome by 2026-10-13: Yellow fever, hepatitis A dose 1, hepatitis B two-dose series, typhoid and influenza vaccines given; certificate valid [S]</item><item>Knows to have a malaria test the same day for any fever, during travel and for months after return. Outcome by 2026-03-31: Carries a fever card and explained the plan back correctly [S]</item></list><paragraph>Immunizations given today: Hepatitis B vaccine (HepB-CpG), dose 2 of 2; Influenza vaccine, inactivated, trivalent, preservative-free [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Guideline basis: CDC Yellow Book: Health Information for International Travel, 2026 edition. Malaria (2026); CDC Yellow Book: Health Information for International Travel, 2026 edition. Yellow Fever (2026); French Guiana (France): travel health destination page (yellow fever and malaria information updated April 2025) (2026); CDC Yellow Book: Health Information for International Travel, 2026 edition. Dengue (2026); CDC Yellow Book: Health Information for International Travel, 2026 edition. Rickettsial Diseases (2026); CDC Yellow Book: Health Information for International Travel, 2026 edition. Travelers&apos; Diarrhea (2026); The practice of travel medicine: guidelines by the Infectious Diseases Society of America (Hill DR, et al. Clin Infect Dis 2006;43(12):1499-1539) (2006)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Short visit a month before departure for the chikungunya vaccine decision. Post-travel visit on return, sooner for any fever. [S]</paragraph></text>

